    bio: 'Vallabhācārya was born in 1479 in the Telugu-speaking Brahmin community of southern India, though his life\'s work unfolded primarily in the Hindi-speaking heartland of Vraja — the sacred landscape associated with the god Krishna around Mathura and Vrindavan. According to tradition, he was born prematurely during a period of great turmoil, when his parents were fleeing the Muslim invasions, and his mother briefly left him behind, believing him stillborn. He was said to have been discovered surrounded by flames, unharmed — a birth narrative that resonated with the divine fire of Krishna\'s grace that would animate his entire philosophical project. From an early age he was recognised as a prodigy, and he undertook extensive pan-Indian pilgrimages, engaging in philosophical debate with scholars of rival traditions and reportedly defeating them at the famous court of Vijayanagara. He was married, had two sons — one of whom, Viṭṭhalanātha, would significantly develop his school — and lived as a householder philosopher-saint rather than as a renunciate, a fact that was itself philosophically significant.\n\nVallabha\'s major philosophical contribution is the system known as Śuddhādvaita — "pure non-dualism" — and his theology of Puṣṭimārga, the "path of grace." His Śuddhādvaita differs from both Śaṅkara\'s Advaita and Rāmānuja\'s Viśiṣṭādvaita in insisting that the world is not illusory (as in Advaita) and not constituted by matter that is distinct from God (as in Viśiṣṭādvaita), but is a real manifestation of Krishna\'s own blissful divine nature. For Vallabha, Brahman is not an impersonal absolute but the personal, supremely blissful Lord Krishna, and the world — including human souls — is Krishna\'s own self-expression, his divine play (līlā). This makes the world not a realm of suffering to be escaped but a theatre of divine delight to be participated in. His major works include the Anubhāṣya (a commentary on the Brahmasūtras), the Tattvārthadīpanibandha, and a cycle of devotional commentaries on the Bhāgavata Purāṇa.\n\nWhat is particularly striking about Vallabha\'s philosophy is the centrality of grace (puṣṭi) over effort or renunciation. In most Indian soteriological systems, liberation is the fruit of sustained personal effort — through knowledge, meditation, or disciplined devotion. Vallabha insists that the soul is so thoroughly caught in worldly attachment that it cannot liberate itself by its own efforts; only Krishna\'s freely given grace can draw the soul into the circle of his divine love. This is not a passive doctrine: it demands an entire way of life oriented toward Krishna\'s pleasure, including the elaborate ritual service of the deity in the home and temple, the offering of music, food, and beauty to the Lord — a practice tradition known as sevā. Vallabha\'s Puṣṭimārga became one of the most artistically fertile religious movements in Indian history, directly inspiring the tradition of classical Braj Bhasha devotional poetry and the development of distinctive schools of painting centred on Krishna\'s divine play.',
